course: dictionary
lesson: 1064
title: A word from a grand house
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to a certain time or place. Boudoir is an old word from French, and you will mostly find it in old stories and in grand houses long ago. Read the words around it to find its meaning. «And the Rat reveals he caught the invitations in time — Mole is now writing plain, simple ones in the blue boudoir (a small, pretty sitting-room).» The brackets give you the answer: a small, pretty sitting-room.
explain: Look at the clues. Mole is writing invitations, and a sitting-room is a place to sit and write quietly. Now try your meaning in the sentence: "Mole is writing invitations in the small, pretty sitting-room" still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: Boudoir is a noun, so it names a thing: a room. It is pronounced BOO-dwah. Both meanings are old-fashioned, so you may rarely hear it today, but you will meet it in books.
fact: A boudoir is a lady's small private sitting room or dressing room.
fact: In literary writing, a boudoir can mean a pretty, private bedroom.
fact: Boudoir comes from French, and it is an old-fashioned word.
fact: Boudoir is pronounced BOO-dwah.
fact: When a writer uses boudoir, the house is often rich and old-fashioned.
exercise: «And the Rat reveals he caught the invitations in time — Mole is now writing plain, simple ones in the blue boudoir.» Where is Mole writing? | *A small, pretty sitting-room | A wet field full of mud | A busy railway station | A noisy kitchen with a big oven
exercise: What is a boudoir? | A bird with blue feathers | A long road into the town | A big kitchen where the food is cooked | *A lady's small private room
exercise: Which kind of house would most likely have a boudoir? | *A grand old house | A boat on a river | A cave under a hill | A tent on a campsite
test: Boudoir is a noun. What does that mean? | It is a question word | *It is a word for a thing, such as a room | It is a word for an action, such as run | It is a word for describing, such as blue
test: Which sentence uses boudoir correctly? | *She wrote her letters in the boudoir, a small pretty room upstairs. | He kicked the boudoir across the field. | We ate boudoirs for lunch. | The boudoir was a loud bus that drove into town.
exam: Jayden says a boudoir is a kitchen. Who is right? | Both are the same, so it does not matter | *Jayden is wrong, because a boudoir is a small private sitting room or dressing room | Nobody knows what it means | Jayden, because kitchens are where people write letters
